Welcome to the Teleport kit. This kit is designed to help you understand, query, and make the most of the audit logs generated by Teleport.

This playbook serves to get you oriented around the kit and provide some anchors into the data and dashboards.

# Getting Data In

See the [Gravwell Teleport Integration Guide](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/teleport.html) for step-by-step File Follower and storage well configuration.

Teleport writes one JSON object per line for every audit event. Reference: [Teleport audit log documentation](https://goteleport.com/docs/reference/deployment/monitoring/audit/).

- **Self-hosted clusters** (default dir backend): the audit log lives under /var/lib/teleport/log/<uuid>/<date>.log on the auth server and rotates roughly daily. Point the Gravwell File Follower at that directory with the JSON timestamp taken from the time field, and assign the teleport-audit tag.
- **Event Handler / Fluentd**: the [Teleport Event Handler](https://goteleport.com/docs/zero-trust-access/export-audit-events/) exports events to Fluentd, which can forward to a Gravwell HTTP or syslog ingester.
- **Cloud-hosted clusters**: Teleport Enterprise Cloud manages audit storage; use the Event Handler to export events.

**Note:** Recorded session content (the replayable terminal streams) is stored separately as protobuf data and is not part of this kit; the audit events *about* sessions are.

# Overview

Every Teleport audit event carries an event type, a short code, a time, and a uid, plus type-specific fields. The event types this kit works with most:

- user.login - authentication to the cluster, with success, method, and the client address in addr.remote
- session.start / session.end / session.join / session.leave - interactive session lifecycle, with sid, server_hostname, and the OS login
- exec - remote command execution, with command and exitCode
- scp / sftp - file transfers, with path and action; legacy SCP transfers emit scp events while Web UI and SFTP transfers emit sftp events, one per file operation
- user.create / user.delete, role.created / role.updated / role.deleted - identity administration
- join_token.create - tokens that let new nodes join the cluster
- access_request.create - requests for elevated roles
- lock.created - locks that block users or nodes
- session.recording.access - replay of recorded sessions

Some Teleport field names contain a literal dot (for example addr.remote). The kit's queries quote these names, and you should too when writing your own:

```
tag=$TELEPORT_AUDIT ax event=="user.login" success=="false" user "addr.remote"
| table user "addr.remote"
```

# Field Extraction

This kit ships a JSON autoextractor bound to the teleport-audit tag, and queries reference fields through the ax module. Note that JSON boolean fields such as success are compared as strings: success=="true" or success=="false".

The roles field is a JSON array and renders as ["access","editor"] in tables. To expand it into one entry per role, append | json -x roles.

# Queries
Queries are broken out into 2 types and have appropriate labels applied for reference.

## Stats
Primarily used by dashboards to generate metrics and provide an overview of activity within the Teleport cluster.

## Detections
Queries built to detect specific behavior that can be indicative of malicious activity: failed login spikes, logins from multiple IPs, user and role manipulation, join token creation, lock creation, session recording access, suspicious remote commands, and file transfer bursts. These can be used ad hoc, serve as a basis for your own detection engineering, or enabled as scheduled searches for alerting. Threshold-based detections should be tuned to your environment.

# Scheduled Searches
Every detection ships with a matching scheduled search that runs hourly over the previous two hours of data. The overlapping window accounts for ingest delay, so a threshold detection may alert twice for the same burst. They are **disabled by default** - review the thresholds, then enable the ones you want from the Scheduled Searches page.

# Dashboards
3 dashboards are provided in this kit.

## Teleport - Audit Overview
High-level view of audit activity. Event volume over time by type, event type distribution, top users, top servers, and recent audit events. Useful as the landing page.

## Teleport - Sessions & Activity
Session-level view: session starts over time, remote commands, file transfers, and session recording access.

## Teleport - Security
Security-focused view: login results and failures over time, role changes, suspicious commands, and join token creation.

# Actionables
## Teleport - Investigate IP Address
When an IP address appears in search results, an option under the "Teleport" menu will run the IP investigation template against Teleport audit logs.

## Teleport - Investigate Session
When a session ID (UUID) appears in search results, an option under the "Teleport" menu will show every audit event for that session. The trigger matches any UUID; every Teleport event also carries a uid, and the actionable only returns results when applied to a session ID (sid).

# Templates
- **Teleport - Investigate User** - audit activity for a Teleport user
- **Teleport - Investigate Server** - session activity for a server
- **Teleport - Investigate IP Address** - audit activity from a client address
- **Teleport - Investigate Session** - full timeline of a session by ID

# Macros
One configuration macro controls which tag the kit queries.

- `$TELEPORT_AUDIT` (default teleport-audit)

If your tag differs from the default you must update **both** the macro value **and** the Tag on the teleport-audit autoextractor (Extractors page) - the kit's queries reference fields through the autoextractor, so if it is not bound to your tag, no fields will be extracted and every query in the kit will return empty results.
